From 1c4e5df0ef7048f019821943c50305dbab804f78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VLCKit Aloha Release <devnull@alohabrowser.com>
Date: Tue, 19 May 2026 19:42:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] chromecast: cast.cpp: catch-all guard for intf_sys_t
 constructor

aloha07 onwards: opening Downloads no longer crashes the input thread.
But picking a Chromecast device in the picker still terminates the
app on iPhone 15:

  libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type
  std::runtime_error: Failed to create client session

The throw site is ChromecastCommunication's constructor when
vlc_tls_SocketOpenTLS() returns NULL after a failed TLS handshake.
That constructor runs from inside intf_sys_t::intf_sys_t() which is
in turn called from cast.cpp's Open(). Open() *does* wrap the call
in try { ... } catch (const std::runtime_error&) { ... } catch
(const std::bad_alloc&) { ... }, so the exception should be caught
and the module should report a graceful failure.

It isn't, because patches 0017/0018 compile libvlc + every contrib
with -fvisibility=hidden. Apple clang treats the typeinfo / vtable
symbols for std::runtime_error as local to each .o that throws or
catches it. The catch block's typed match against
std::runtime_error then fails for an exception thrown from a
different TU -- their type_info pointers are not identical even
though the type is. The exception escapes Open(), unwinds out to
the stream-out worker thread root, hits no handler, and
terminate() kills the app.

The clean fix would be to drop -fvisibility=hidden for type_info
symbols (or for the chromecast TUs specifically), but doing that
re-opens the FFmpeg symbol-collision problem that the visibility
patches were added to close, and risks breaking the very fragile
ld -r repack pipeline.

A surgical catch (...) at the throw site's nearest synchronous
caller is safe: it catches the exception regardless of typeinfo
identity, logs a generic failure, and routes through the existing
error cleanup path exactly like the typed std::runtime_error
branch above. Behaviour is identical to a successful typed catch:
the Chromecast renderer fails to attach, the app stays alive, and
the user can try again or pick a different device.
---
 modules/stream_out/chromecast/cast.cpp | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/modules/stream_out/chromecast/cast.cpp b/modules/stream_out/chromecast/cast.cpp
index 65c312a00e..3085a5194b 100644
--- a/modules/stream_out/chromecast/cast.cpp
+++ b/modules/stream_out/chromecast/cast.cpp
@@ -1312,6 +1312,28 @@ static int Open(vlc_object_t *p_this)
         p_intf = NULL;
         goto error;
     }
+    /* Aloha: catch-all guard. With patches 0017/0018 applied,
+     * libvlc and all contribs are compiled with -fvisibility=hidden, which
+     * also hides C++ typeinfo / vtable symbols. A `throw std::runtime_error`
+     * from chromecast_communication.cpp can then fail to match the typed
+     * `catch (const std::runtime_error&)` above, because the type_info
+     * objects in the throw TU and the catch TU are no longer the same
+     * (both are local to their own .o file). The exception then propagates
+     * to the worker thread root and `terminate()` kills the app:
+     *
+     *   libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type
+     *   std::runtime_error: Failed to create client session
+     *
+     * Catch (...) here is a guaranteed match regardless of how the
+     * type_info symbols ended up linked. Behaviour is the same as the
+     * typed `std::runtime_error` branch: log a generic failure and goto
+     * the error cleanup path. */
+    catch (...)
+    {
+        msg_Err( p_this, "cannot load the Chromecast controller (unknown error)" );
+        p_intf = NULL;
+        goto error;
+    }
 
     b_supports_video = var_GetBool(p_stream, SOUT_CFG_PREFIX "video");
 
